t baker collapsed on the ground  still alive  the defendant exits the vehicle out of  the driver's side builder which he  previously said was broken he approaches  sergeant baker and at close range  executes  this is on video  prosecutors say the defendant that then  got into baker's  patrol vehicle drove it next to baker's  body loaded into the vehicle  and drove away  the defendant pretending to be law  enforcement answers  multiple radio calls from the dixon  county central dispatch  he's thinking clearly and quickly and he  responds  10-4 one in custody in route  the defendant answered sergeant baker's  cell phone  a call from deputy chris lashley  and he tells chris lashley pretending to  be sergeant-bacon  10-4 everything's okay he exits the  roadway  and he drives through a couple of fields  to a remote area that can't be seen from  the road  he gets out of the vehicle the defendant  then  sets fire to sergeant baker  and also the front seat of the bar he  closes the doors  locks the doors and flees  the defendant was arrested after two  days following one of the largest  manhunts in the state of tennessee  prosecutors say he was found in  possession of the murder weapon  and voluntarily gave a statement to  investigators  in that statement  he was asked why  did you walk up and shoot sergeant baker  in the head  close range and the defendant said  it's like a dog you don't let it suffer  when we come back the victim's widow  takes the stand the heartbreaking  testimony about the loss of a husband  father and law enforcement officer next  [Music]  back now to the trial of an accused cop  killer stephen wiggins  his defense attorney admitted his client  is the real killer but  pleading with the jury that he was not  premeditated  the proof in this case is gonna make you  happy  it's gonna make you angry and i know  that because it made me  and it wasn't until i was able to  learn more about stephen his life  history and his life course  that i was even able to begin to fight  how something like this could happen the  defense  does not intend to put forth witnesses  in this portion of the case  our intention is to cross examine  witnesses only to the extent  necessary to complete a picture  prosecutors say the defendant also stole  baker's backup weapon  a pistol his wife bought him for  christmas  one of the first well the first year we  were together um  i got him a a glock 357.  okay it so what did  daniel do with the glock 357 is that  something  it was to be used as a backup weapon  okay uh and  and would you see him carry it on a  daily basis as his backup weapon  yes i would see him with it um on  several occasions sometimes it stayed in  his police car so i  i didn't see it every day but i knew  that he had it how did you know that he  wanted a  357 as a backup weapon when there was  something that daniel wanted  then he made it known and he would drop  pins here and there too  i knew that's what he wanted he even had  it lined up at the  sportsman's store you know if i could if  she comes in this is the one i want  well did you attempt to fool him that  christmas as to  yes because that was i did that every  year  but um i would wrap something and if it  was small i would  wrap it in several sizes boxes  and i even put bricks in this one to  make it you know to try to mask what it  is  let's bring back civil rights attorney  katie smith and public defender honor  carlson  katie in the prosecution's opening they  said wiggins referred to the victim as a  dog  that he had to put him out of his misery  how much of an emotional impact do you  think this is going to have  on the jury during the openings brian i  think it's a huge emotional impact  you know it really shows the way he was  thinking about the victim at that time  and it's  really not doing any favors to the  defense  and i think immediately when you hear  that it feels so callous and the jury is  not supposed to of course consider  certain emotional aspects to things but  the jury is human  and hearing that is very disturbing and  it certainly goes to  this ultimate issue about whether or not  he really intended the killing  and i think that is not a good fact for  the defense yeah that dog statement  definitely is going to come back to bite  him because that shows intense in some  level of premeditation i think  anna the defense said they wouldn't  cross certain witnesses  and that they would not dishonor the  victim's memory your thoughts on the  defense's tactic here  so brian when i heard about this tactic  at first i thought that they were  referring to not wanting to  cross-examine police officers or other  potential witnesses in the case which i  think  in most cases would be a mistake you  almost never want  don't want to ask no questions you want  to maximize the impact of  one well-timed question their defense  here is  regarding premeditation regarding  whether he  um he had planned this and so asking a  police officer  well you never met stephen wiggins  before this you know before this  incident  or you weren't present on that day just  that one single question can have impact  but after watching uh the testimony from  what they call the spark of life witness  that's the the widow lisa baker  i think he's referring to reassuring the  jury he's not going to cross-examine the  grieving widow  and there really is nothing that that he  could accomplish by doing that  yeah i think it made a lot of sense i  think uh to some degree when you don't  have the facts on your side  as many defense attorneys often have in  cases  trying to find a middle ground with the  jury and say hey i'm not going to  overstep  my balance but i'm going to do my job  here i think that's a very smart move  thank you anna and katie and thank you  for joining us here on law and crime  daily  we'll see you next time as we discuss  justice in america  [Music]  you
 

